WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.580
Frozen was a sensation when it hit theaters
in 2013.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:10.470
The tale of two sisters trapped in a perpetual
winter was an awards season darling and a

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.660
major box-office smash.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:25.540
Now, the highly-anticipated sequel is expected
to hit theaters on November 27th, 2019.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:30.119
Disney is tight-lipped on details, but some
juicy tidbits have managed to slip through

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:31.229
the icy cracks.

00:00:31.229 --> 00:00:35.989
Here are all the Frozen 2 rumors and spoilers
so far.

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:37.829
Together again

00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:40.960
It wouldn't be Frozen without Princess Elsa
and Princess Anna.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.410
The vocal talent behind both royals, Idina
Menzel and Kristen Bell, are returning for

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:47.410
the sequel.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.999
They've even recorded many of their lines
already.

00:00:49.999 --> 00:00:54.980
Anna's love interest Kristoff is also back
in business, with Jonathan Groff confirming

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:57.539
he's returned to the recording studio.

00:00:57.539 --> 00:01:02.760
The wacky snowman Olaf, a fan favorite voiced
by Josh Gad, will be on hand, too.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.670
Alan Tudyk, who voiced The Duke of Weselton
in the original film, is coming back in a

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:08.670
big way.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:14.350
A staple of Disney and Pixar films, Tudyk
previously lent his vocal stylings to films

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:17.640
like Wreck It Ralph, Moana, and Zootopia.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.939
He told SlashFilm he's coming back, but isn't
sure what character he'll be playing.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:24.720
That answer sounds… rather weaselly.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:26.800
"Weselton!

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:27.740
It's Weselton!"

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:31.110
A girlfriend for Elsa?

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:35.920
Some fans have campaigned for the sequel to
include a female love interest for Elsa.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.909
Co-director and screenwriter Jennifer Lee
gave those fans some hope by telling The Huffington

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:41.909
Post,

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:44.260
"[There have been] tons of conversations about
it.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:45.570
[…] We'll see where we go."

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:50.840
While appearing on The Tyler Oakley Show,
actress Idina Menzel even had some ideas about

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:52.380
who Elsa's love interest might be:

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.220
"You can't help who you have a connection
with."

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:55.980
"Tinkerbell?

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:56.480
Hahahaha."

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.640
"Tinkerbell, that's cute."

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.149
The sisters: A deeper look

00:02:01.149 --> 00:02:06.229
To prepare for the sequel, Jennifer Lee sat
down and wrote journal entries as both sisters.

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:10.780
She even had a psychologist psychoanalyze
Anna and Elsa while conceiving the Broadway

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:16.100
musical version of Frozen, so she must have
the answers to some burning, or freezing,

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.100
questions:

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.250
"What did I ever do to you?"

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:20.250
"Enough, Anna."

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.250
"No!

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:22.250
Why, why do you shut me out?

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:24.460
Why do you shut the world out?"

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:29.640
Songwriter Kristen Anderson-Lopez says many
of these revelations will be reflected in

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:31.170
the new music, too.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:32.170
She tells Variety:

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.090
"We know the girls a lot better now.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:37.080
We've gone a lot deeper into their heads."

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.590
The parents are back… somehow

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:44.230
Anderson-Lopez also claims the filmmakers
got to know Elsa and Anna's parents a lot

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.580
better in the process of writing the next
movie.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:51.620
That means the sequel may explore why the
parents locked Elsa away, causing the deep

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:53.110
rift between the two sisters.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:58.030
One of the more outlandish rumors suggests
we'll get to know the parents better.. .because

00:02:58.030 --> 00:02:59.960
they aren't really dead.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.680
Some unsubstantiated reports suggest they
might not have died in that shipwreck, although

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.200
it's more likely that we'll learn more about
the parents through flashbacks or through

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.040
memories from Anna and Elsa.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:12.860
Elsa's fun side

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:16.890
Throughout Frozen, Elsa came to terms with
her powers and tried to figure out a way to

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:18.670
use them without hurting anybody.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:23.450
While her journey led to lots of incredible
drama, it didn't leave much room for lighter

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:24.140
moments.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:25.140
"How?

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:27.580
What power do you have to stop this winter?

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:29.050
To stop me…?"

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:34.290
That changed with the animated shorts Frozen
Fever and Olaf's Frozen Adventure, which let

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.480
Elsa show off her lighter side.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.090
Co-director Chris Buck told Fandango he loved
making the shorts because he could have more

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:42.450
fun with Elsa.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:46.950
He added that the sequel will go down a similar
path, saying that we'd see more of her personality

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:48.730
come out in the next movie.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:54.090
We loved Frozen's drama, but it'll be fun
watching Elsa break the ice by cracking jokes,

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:55.090
too.

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:56.530
The hidden folk

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:02.120
In Frozen, Anna and Elsa encountered a group
of noble troll people who helped them understand

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.120
magic.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.290
But the Broadway musical version of the film
dropped the troll people in favor of a new

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.600
group of characters: the hidden folk.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.030
Well, the hidden folk may be moving from the
stage to the big screen in Frozen 2.

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:17.819
The show's co-songwriter Kristen Anderson-Lopez
told Variety:

00:04:17.819 --> 00:04:20.410
"We've thought a lot more about those hidden
folk.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.129
[...] That's all I'm going to say."

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:25.080
Strange Magic?

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:29.219
Frozen 2 has been the tentative title for
the upcoming film, but one rumor suggests

00:04:29.219 --> 00:04:32.550
the sequel will be called Frozen: Strange
Magic.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:37.599
Disney registered several domain names related
to Strange Magic, which seems to support this

00:04:37.599 --> 00:04:38.599
theory.

00:04:38.599 --> 00:04:42.889
The term was used in an ad for the first film,
and spoken by The Duke of Weselton:

00:04:42.889 --> 00:04:45.300
"Strange magic has frozen this land."

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.530
"Now we just have to survive this blizzard!"

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:52.240
It seems likely Disney will want to use a
title that's a bit more artful than Frozen

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:55.610
2, and Strange Magic certainly seems apt.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:58.770
Of course, we'll just have to wait and see
what the official title will be.

